---
/* One recorded run, in full: the session, what it teaches, and what was
 * checked while it ran.
 *
 * The checks are the difference between this and a screencast. Each one is a
 * claim the scenario file makes -- a command's exit status, a condition read as
 * type, status and reason on a live object -- and the recording exists only
 * because every one of them held.
 */
import StarlightPage from '@astrojs/starlight/components/StarlightPage.astro';
import RunFrame from '../../components/RunFrame.astro';
import { Runs, playerCatalog, runSummary } from '../../lib/runs.mjs';

export function getStaticPaths() {
	return Runs.map((run) => ({ params: { run: run.id }, props: { run } }));
}

const { run } = Astro.props;
const catalog = playerCatalog();
const lab = runSummary(run);
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<StarlightPage
	frontmatter={{
		title: run.title,
		description: run.tagline,
		tableOfContents: false,
	}}
>
	<p class="ptah-demo-lede">{run.tagline}</p>
	<p class="ptah-demo-learn"><strong>What it shows.</strong> {run.learn}</p>
	{run.start && <p class="ptah-demo-learn"><strong>Where it starts.</strong> {run.start}</p>}

	<p class="ptah-demo-replay">
		A replay of a verified run, not a live cluster. {run.checks.length}
		{run.checks.length === 1 ? ' check held' : ' checks held'} while it was recorded.{' '}
		<strong>Play</strong> types it out in the frame below; the transcript is the same session, already there.
	</p>

	<RunFrame id={run.id} title={run.title} events={run.events} />

	<h2>What was checked</h2>
	<p>
		Every step states what has to hold before its output may be published. A condition is read as its
		type, status and reason on the live object, never as a phrase in a message.
	</p>
	<div class="ptah-demo-checks">
		<table>
			<thead>
				<tr><th>Step</th><th>Claim</th><th>Held</th></tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody>
				{run.checks.map((check) => (
					<tr>
						<td>{check.step}</td>
						<td><code>{check.claim}</code></td>
						<td>{check.passed ? 'yes' : 'no'}</td>
					</tr>
				))}
			</tbody>
		</table>
	</div>

	<h2>What it ran against</h2>
	<ul class="ptah-demo-lab">
		{lab.map((one) => (
			<li><span class="ptah-demo-lab-label">{one.label}</span><span class="ptah-demo-lab-value">{one.value}</span></li>
		))}
	</ul>

	<h2>What the commands read</h2>
	<p>
		A session is repeated, not admired, so every name in it is one your own environment can carry.
		There is nothing else: no helper of ours stands between a command and the cluster, and the
		recorder refuses a step that reads a name this list does not hold. The commands are
		<code>kubectl</code>, <code>ptah</code> and <code>kubectl ptah</code>, each of which you
		install once; the last one is <a href={`${base}use/read-a-plan/#install`}>the operator&rsquo;s
		own client</a>. They run from the root of a clone of this repository, which holds the files they
		name: the SQL under <code>demo/schemas</code>, <code>demo/migrations</code>,
		<code>demo/migrations-checkpoint</code> and <code>demo/mysql</code>, the reference data under
		<code>demo/reference</code>, and the manifests under <code>demo/manifests</code>.
	</p>
	<ul class="ptah-demo-lab">
		<li><span class="ptah-demo-lab-label">KUBECONFIG</span><span class="ptah-demo-lab-value">the cluster the commands run against</span></li>
		<li><span class="ptah-demo-lab-label">NAMESPACE</span><span class="ptah-demo-lab-value">the namespace the resources and their database credentials live in</span></li>
		<li><span class="ptah-demo-lab-label">OPERATOR_NAMESPACE</span><span class="ptah-demo-lab-value">where the chart installed the operator</span></li>
		<li><span class="ptah-demo-lab-label">CONTROLLER</span><span class="ptah-demo-lab-value">the controller Deployment&rsquo;s name, from that release</span></li>
		<li><span class="ptah-demo-lab-label">EXECUTOR_IMAGE</span><span class="ptah-demo-lab-value">the executor image the chart runs, its <code>executorImage</code> value</span></li>
		<li><span class="ptah-demo-lab-label">REGISTRY_IN_CLUSTER</span><span class="ptah-demo-lab-value">the registry address a Pod in the cluster resolves</span></li>
		<li><span class="ptah-demo-lab-label">PTAH_OCI_REGISTRY</span><span class="ptah-demo-lab-value">the registry address the push goes to</span></li>
		<li><span class="ptah-demo-lab-label">PTAH_OCI_USERNAME</span><span class="ptah-demo-lab-value">the account that push uses, read by <code>ptah</code> itself</span></li>
		<li><span class="ptah-demo-lab-label">PTAH_OCI_PASSWORD</span><span class="ptah-demo-lab-value">its password, which no command prints</span></li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		The last two are how a CI job hands <code>ptah</code> a registry credential. On a workstation,
		<code>ptah oci login</code> stores it once instead, reading the password from a prompt or from
		standard input rather than from the command line.
	</p>
	<p>
		The registry has two addresses, the way a registry in an ordinary deployment does: a push goes
		to <code>PTAH_OCI_REGISTRY</code>, and the resource names <code>REGISTRY_IN_CLUSTER</code> with
		the same repository and digest. The registry Secret a resource names says which address its
		credential is for, and the operator sends it to no other.
	</p>
	<p>
		The lab itself appears in one command, <code>demo/bin/lab versions</code> in Stand up the lab,
		which says what the lab is made of. What a run starts from is its preparation, which is not
		recorded; <strong>Where it starts</strong> above says what that is and which run ends there. Run
		<code>demo/acceptance/reproduce.sh</code> to watch Approve a plan repeated from a directory where
		the lab&rsquo;s scripts do not exist, by an account that may not create a Job, with the plan
		approved by a second account that may create neither a schema nor a Job.
	</p>

	<h2>The same changes, as edits</h2>
	<p>
		Each <code>kubectl ptah</code> command that changes a resource changes one field of it, so the
		same change is an ordinary edit, with <code>kubectl edit ptahschema storefront -n "$NAMESPACE"</code>
		or in a manifest you apply; the runs that keep their resources as files under
		<code>demo/manifests</code> make their changes that way. What the commands add is checking:
		set-source takes only a reference pinned by digest, changes nothing while an Apply may be
		running, and changes nothing another writer changed since it read the resource.
	</p>
	{/* Not in .ptah-demo-checks: check-demo-page counts that table's rows as
		the run's checks. */}
	<table>
		<thead>
			<tr><th>Command</th><th>The line it changes</th></tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
			<tr><td><code>kubectl ptah suspend</code></td><td><code>spec.suspend: true</code></td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>kubectl ptah resume</code></td><td><code>spec.suspend: false</code></td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>kubectl ptah set-source --oci</code></td><td><code>spec.desired.ociRef</code> of a schema, <code>spec.artifact.ociRef</code> of a migration</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>kubectl ptah refresh</code></td><td>the annotation <code>operator.ptah.run/read-requested-at</code>, set to the time of the request</td></tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>
	<p>
		An approval is not one of them. It is an object of its own, created once by the person who
		decided: <code>kubectl ptah approve --dry-run client</code> prints it as YAML for a repository, to
		be created with <code>kubectl create</code>, and never edited afterwards.
	</p>

	<p>
		The scenario is <code>demo/scenarios/{run.id}.yaml</code>, and <code>make demo</code> replays the
		whole set against a lab of your own. <a href={`${base}demo/`}>All recorded runs</a>.
	</p>

</StarlightPage>

<script is:inline set:html={`window.PTAH_RUNS = ${JSON.stringify(catalog)};`} />
<script type="module" src={`${base}demo/player.js`}></script>
